1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 Bg4 $2 4. dxe5 Bxf3 {White's queen on d1 cannot be
adequately defended. It is attacked by Black's bishop on f3 but defended only by
White's king on e1. A capture sequence here wins roughly a queen. Meanwhile,
Black's bishop on f3 hangs — nobody defends it. It is under attack from
White's queen on d1 and the pawn on g2.} 5. Qxf3 dxe5 6. Bc4 Nf6 7. Qb3 Qe7 8.
Nc3 c6 {White has the superior minor pieces. White owns the bishop pair in an
open position. A good plan for White: open the position so the bishops can
breathe.} 9. Bg5 b5 $4 {White's bishop on c4 has more attackers than its
defenders can handle. The attack from Black's pawn on b5 is met only by White's
queen on b3. If the captures start, the attacker comes out ahead by about two
pawns. Meanwhile, White's queen on b3 is running out of squares — it has no
safe retreat, and the trap is ready to be sprung. Only White's pawn on a2, the
pawn on c2 and the bishop on c4 keep it defended.} 10. Nxb5 {White's knight on
b5 is under pressure — its defense is stretched thin. It is attacked by
Black's pawn on c6 but defended only by White's queen on b3 and the bishop on
c4. A capture sequence here wins about two pawns.} 10... cxb5 11. Bxb5+ {White
has the healthier pawn structure. White has a passed pawn on c2. Black's a7-pawn
is isolated. White has a queenside pawn majority. A good plan for White: advance
the queenside pawn majority. Another idea for Black: blockade White's passed
pawn (key squares: c3).} 11... Nbd7 {Black's knight on d7 is trapped — it has
no safe square to run to. It is attacked by White's bishop on b5 but defended
only by Black's knight on f6, the queen on e7 and the king on e8.} 12. O-O-O
{White's king is not entirely safe around c1. The d-file in front of the king is
wide open.} 12... Rd8 13. Rxd7 {Black's queen on e7 cannot be adequately
defended. It is attacked by White's rook on d7 but defended only by Black's king
on e8 and the bishop on f8. A capture sequence here wins roughly a rook.
Meanwhile, White's rook on d7 cannot be adequately defended. It is attacked by
Black's knight on f6, the queen on e7, the rook on d8 and the king on e8 but
defended only by White's bishop on b5. White has the initiative and is dictating
play.} 13... Rxd7 {Black is ahead on material. Black is three pawns up.} 14. Rd1
Qe6 15. Bxd7+ {The defense of Black's queen on e6 simply does not hold together.
The attack from White's queen on b3 and the bishop on d7 is met only by Black's
pawn on f7. If the captures start, the attacker comes out ahead by roughly a
queen. Meanwhile, Black's queen on e6 is trapped — it has no safe square to
run to.} 15... Nxd7 16. Qb8+ {White's queen on b8 hangs — nobody defends it.
It is under attack from Black's knight on d7.} 16... Nxb8 {Black has a winning
material advantage. Black is up a decisive amount of material.} 17. Rd8# 1-0
